SHAMSU — the pinned prompt, the telemetry toolbar, the side dispatcher,
         and the task queue
=======================================================================
2026-08-22 · branch small-shamsu-new
Model for the live run: qwen2.5-coder:3b-instruct (per CLAUDE.md §5)

Console fixes 01-04 from the "Harness and Console" upgrade spec
(artifact 1cc2a4ba). Harness fixes 05-08 are NOT in this change.


THE CONSTRAINT THAT TURNED OUT NOT TO EXIST
-------------------------------------------
Fix 01 (a pinned input box) was scoped out of the earlier CLI work on the
grounds that it "will fatally conflict with the msvcrt raw keystroke thread on
Windows". That is true and it is the wrong conclusion, because the keystroke
thread is ours.

    pyproject.toml:8    "prompt_toolkit>=3.0"     already a dependency
    .venv               prompt_toolkit 3.0.53      already installed
    repl.py:32          PromptSession              already the idle prompt
    safety/approval.py                             already the approval prompt
    control/console.py                             already the control console

prompt_toolkit already owned every input surface in SHAMSU except one: forty
lines of raw `msvcrt.getwch()` in `_LiveFeedbackReader`, which was the sole
reason the conflict existed. The reader is deleted. There is no rich.Live here
and no raw thread either.


WHAT THE OLD READER ACTUALLY DID, line by line
----------------------------------------------
    char = msvcrt.getwch()          # no echo - nothing on screen
    elif char == chr(8):            # backspace pops a buffer you cannot see
        if buffer: buffer.pop()
    ...
    self._queue.push(text)          # EVERY keystroke goes to the model

Fix 01's complaint ("no cursor, no visible line, nothing until you press Enter
and hope") and fix 03's complaint ("asking how full is my context costs context
to ask") are both visible in that one function.


WHAT WAS BUILT
--------------
`shamsu/cli/live_console.py` — TurnTelemetry, route_input, LiveConsole,
ToolbarStatus.

01  PINNED INPUT   One PromptSession on the REPL's own event loop, running as a
                   task alongside the turn, under `patch_stdout(raw=True)` so
                   the agent's log scrolls above it. Real echo, real cursor,
                   real backspace, real history, and whatever is half-typed
                   survives the output that lands underneath it.

02  TELEMETRY      prompt_toolkit's own `bottom_toolbar` - already a pinned,
                   refreshable region, so nothing fights for the bottom line.
                   `console.status` retires with the reader; the spinner moves
                   into the toolbar and `refresh_interval` drives it.

03  DISPATCHER     `route_input()`. A leading `/` runs locally and prints; it
                   never enters the message array. MIDTURN_COMMANDS is
                   deliberately three entries - a name on the whitelist with no
                   handler behind it is a promise the prompt cannot keep.

04  DUAL QUEUE     `TaskQueue` beside `FeedbackQueue`. Plain text still
                   interrupts at the next round boundary. `/queue add <task>`
                   waits, and `main()` takes it as the next prompt itself.

NOT BUILT: the full-height right sidebar. A side pane means owning the frame
with an Application and a Layout, and that costs the scrollback. The toolbar
carries the same numbers at the bottom of a terminal that still scrolls.


THE TOOLBAR, AND WHY IT NARROWS FROM THE LEFT
---------------------------------------------
    ⠋ read_file config.py                                                  1m04s
    rnd 14/24 │ ctx ███████░░░ 71% 23.3k/32.8k │ files config.py │ contracts 1 │ fb 1  tasks 1

First version simply clipped the row to the terminal width. Clipping trims the
END, and the end is where the queue depths are - so on a narrow terminal the
first thing to disappear was the number telling you the steer you had just
typed had been accepted. That is the exact failure the pinned prompt exists to
fix.

It now gives ground from the left, cheapest information first, measured live:

     110 | rnd 14/24 │ ctx ███████░░░ 71% 23.3k/32.8k │ files config.py js/PlayerShip.js │ contracts 1 │ fb 2  tasks 1
     100 | rnd 14/24 │ ctx ███████░░░ 71% 23.3k/32.8k │ files 2 files │ contracts 1 │ fb 2  tasks 1
      80 | rnd 14/24 │ ctx ███████░░░ 71% 23.3k/32.8k │ contracts 1 │ fb 2  tasks 1
      70 | rnd 14/24 │ ctx ███████░░░ 71% │ ct 1 │ fb 2  tasks 1
      50 | rnd 14/24 │ ctx 71% │ ct 1 │ fb 2  tasks 1
      44 | rnd 14/24 │ ctx 71% │ ct 1 │ fb 2  tasks 1

The round budget and both queue depths survive to 44 columns.


HANDING THE TERMINAL TO AN APPROVAL PROMPT
------------------------------------------
Two prompt_toolkit applications cannot share a console, and the approval prompt
is one. The first version watched `prompt_is_active()` on a poll, which leaves
a window: the flag goes up, and until the next tick both are live.

`safety/approval.py` now has an observer list. `reading_input()` calls it
BEFORE the question is rendered, so the handover is synchronous:

    _PROMPT_DEPTH += 1
    for callback in list(_ON_PROMPT_OPEN):   # <- live console stands down here
        with contextlib.suppress(Exception): callback()
    yield                                     # <- then the question is drawn

Tools run on a worker thread, so `LiveConsole.stand_down` does not touch the
app: it hands `app.exit()` to the loop the prompt is running on via
`call_soon_threadsafe`. Rendering the approval panel is what buys the loop the
moment it needs to act on it, and the poll stays as a backstop at 10ms for
anything that takes the terminal without announcing itself.

A broken observer is suppressed. It must never be the reason a human cannot be
asked to approve a write.


